# Code owners for aksh.
#
# Every pull request targeting a protected branch requires an approving review
# from at least one of the people listed here. GitHub does not let an author
# approve their own pull request, so a change written by one owner must be
# reviewed by one of the others.
#
# A code owner's approval only counts if they have write access to the
# repository. Keep this list in sync with the collaborator list -- an entry for
# someone without write access is silently ignored, which would weaken the
# review requirement without any visible error on the pull request.

# Default owners for everything in the repository. Later patterns override
# earlier ones, so this must stay first for the rules below to take effect.
*       @girishmotwani @mugeshsp @ritwikranjan

# Security-relevant surfaces are called out explicitly rather than relying on
# the catch-all above. The owners are identical today, so these lines do not
# change who may approve; they exist so that narrowing ownership later cannot
# accidentally leave these paths unowned, and so reviewers can see at a glance
# which parts of the tree carry enforcement guarantees.

# Policy evaluation and the enforcement decision path.
/internal/policy/       @girishmotwani @mugeshsp @ritwikranjan

# The AkshPolicy API types. Changes here are a compatibility surface for every
# cluster that has already applied a policy.
/api/                   @girishmotwani @mugeshsp @ritwikranjan

# TLS termination, connection capture, and the upstream dialler.
/internal/dataplane/    @girishmotwani @mugeshsp @ritwikranjan

# eBPF C sources and the generated object. Changes here alter what the kernel
# executes and are not reviewable from the Go code alone.
/internal/dataplane/bpf/ @girishmotwani @mugeshsp @ritwikranjan

# Credential brokering and injection.
/internal/token/        @girishmotwani @mugeshsp @ritwikranjan

# Certificate authority and leaf issuance used to terminate TLS.
/internal/pki/          @girishmotwani @mugeshsp @ritwikranjan

# The per-request audit record, which is the evidence trail for enforcement.
/internal/audit/        @girishmotwani @mugeshsp @ritwikranjan

# CI, release automation, and this ownership file itself. Without this line a
# change to CODEOWNERS or to a workflow would be reviewable under the default
# rule alone; keeping it explicit makes tampering with the review process
# visible as a change to an owned path.
/.github/               @girishmotwani @mugeshsp @ritwikranjan

# Admission webhook and deployment manifests, which control whether the sidecar
# is injected at all.
/deploy/                @girishmotwani @mugeshsp @ritwikranjan

# Container build definitions for the published images.
/build/                 @girishmotwani @mugeshsp @ritwikranjan

